  "subject": "🫙 Axios Finish Line: Bottling life",
  "date": "Tue, 13 Oct 2026 21:32:56 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🥪 Plus: Kindness at lunch | Tuesday, October 13, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 13, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Axios national energy correspondent Amy Harder is guest-hosting tonight, reflecting on lessons from loss.\n\n Smart Brevity™count: 584 words … 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Trying to bottle up life and its lessons \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Amy Harder writes: \n\n Death has a way of teaching us about living — if only life wouldn't get in the way.\n\n Why it matters: Losing someone can bring sudden clarity to what actually matters. But making that change in perspective stick is hard. My mother-in-law recently passed away, and almost immediately I wanted to bottle up everything our relationship and her death taught me.\n\n These lessons — like making time for what matters, saying how you feel while you have the chance and taking more videos — are so obvious that they're easy to ignore until death makes them impossible to. 👓 The big picture: Death brings the fragility of life into sharp focus.\n\n I suddenly wanted to hug everyone in my family as tightly as possible and somehow make all of us understand, right now, that we need to ensure we're making the most of our time together. Reality check: Intellectually understanding that life is finite doesn't mean we can permanently feel the urgency of that fact. Yes, there's a temporary bump in togetherness, but then something maddening happens: Life goes on.\n\n Jobs consume most of our waking hours during the week. Trips and other life obligations compete for our time. Sometimes we're simply tired and want to watch dumb TV shows. The profound realization that life is short somehow has to coexist with needing to unload the dishwasher. 💡 What I'm learning: Maybe I can't bottle up that feeling of urgency forever. But I don't have to preserve it at full strength for the experience to change me.\n\n I want more videos and audio recordings of the people I love, particularly parents who may come second or third on the camera roll after children and pets. I recently asked my mother to leave me a happy birthday voice message. Now I'll have it forever. I want to keep showing up for the big moments while appreciating more of the utterly unremarkable ones: a grocery store trip, a random lunch, a phone conversation about nothing. ⏳ Yes, but: I want to be kinder to myself about the moments I will inevitably miss.\n\n I've caught myself regretting time I didn't spend with loved ones, but I can't possibly be everywhere or do everything. The moments I miss don't diminish the ones I can make time for. The bottom line : Death reminds us to live to the fullest. Life reminds us we can't always do that.\n\n Share this column . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Find where your team is misaligned \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Simplify Workshop shows your team where it is misaligned and why it keeps happening.\n\n Everyone leaves with one aligned set of priorities and a real kill list, built on the Confront, Delete, Amplify method. The whole team works through it together.\n\n Learn more about the Workshop \n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🍸 Lunch on me! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n We love reading about the random acts of kindness Finish Line readers are encountering across the country. Here's one from Jan Lincoln of Mesa, Ariz.:\n\n \"While my husband and I were waiting at an outdoor table at a cafe in Stanley, Idaho, I noticed a woman next to me reading and lunching alone. I wanted to know what she was reading, so I went to ask her. Turns out she was a bartender, and I'm a hobbyist mixologist.\" \"We talked cocktails for a bit until my order arrived. My husband and I enjoyed our lunch. Our neighbor departed with a nod and a wave. When we went to pay, we found out she'd paid for our lunch.\" We'll keep featuring these nuggets of good news through the holiday season and beyond! Hit us at finishline@axios.com with your random act of kindness, plus your name and hometown.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
